## Line Integrals {#sec-9-8}

* Integration of a function defined along a curve

![](figures/ch09_figure17.svg "various curve"){width="95%" fig-align="center"}

* Definite Integral

  $$\lim_{\|P\|\to 0} \sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k^*)\Delta x_k = \int_a^b f(x)\,dx$$

![](figures/ch09_figure18.svg "sample point in k"){width="70%" fig-align="center"}

* Line Integrals in the Plane

![](figures/ch09_figure19.svg "sample point in k subarc"){width="45%" fig-align="center"}

$$
\begin{aligned}
    \lim_{\| P \|\to 0} \sum_{k=1}^n G(x_k^*, y_k^*)\,\Delta x_k &= \int_C G(x,y)\,dx\\ 
    \lim_{\| P \|\to 0} \sum_{k=1}^n G(x_k^*, y_k^*)\,\Delta y_k &=\int_C G(x,y)\,dy\\
    \lim_{\| P \|\to 0} \sum_{k=1}^n G(x_k^*, y_k^*)\,\Delta s_k &= \int_C G(x,y)\,ds
\end{aligned}$$

* Method of Evaluation 

  * Curve Defined Parametrically, $\;x=f(t)$, $\;y=g(t)$, $\;a\leq t \leq b$ 

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
      \int_C G(x,y)\,dx 
       &=\int_a^b G(\,f(t), g(t)) \,f'(t)\,dt \\ 
      \int_C G(x,y)\,dy 
       &=\int_a^b G(\,f(t), g(t)) \,g'(t)\,dt \\
      \int_C G(x,y)\,ds 
       &=\int_a^b G(\,f(t), g(t)) \,\sqrt{[f'(t)]^2 
       +[g'(t)]^2}\,dt
    \end{aligned}$$

  * Curve Defined by an Explicit Function, $\;y=f(x)$, $\;a\leq x \leq b$ 

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
      \int_C G(x,y)\,dx 
       &=\int_a^b G(x, f(x)) \,dx \\ 
      \int_C G(x,y)\,dy 
       &=\int_a^b G(x, f(x)) \,f'(x)\,dx \\
      \int_C G(x,y)\,ds 
       &=\int_a^b G(x, f(x)) \,\sqrt{1 
        +[f'(x)]^2}\,dx
    \end{aligned}$$

* Notation

  $${\displaystyle\int_C P\,dx +\int_C Q\,dy \,\Rightarrow \, \int_C P\,dx +Q \,dy}$$

  $${\displaystyle\int_{-C} P\,dx +Q\,dy = -\int_C P\,dx +Q\,dy}$$

![](figures/ch09_figure20.svg "opposite_orientation"){width="40%" fig-align="center"}

* A line integral along a closed curve $C$

  $$\oint_C P \,dx +Q \,dy$$

* Line Integrals in Space

  $$
  \begin{aligned}
  \int_C &G(x,y,z)\,ds = \\ &\int_a^b G(\,f(t), g(t),h(t)) \,\sqrt{[f'(t)]^2 +[g'(t)]^2 +[h'(t)]^2}\,dt
  \end{aligned}$$

* Suppose the vector-valued function 

  $$\mathbf{f}(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\mathbf{i} +Q(x,y,z)\mathbf{j} +R(x,y,z)\mathbf{k}$$

  is defined along a curve $C$ and 

  $$d\mathbf{r}=dx\mathbf{i} +dy\mathbf{j} +dz\mathbf{k}\,$$ 

  is the displacement vector of points on $C$, $\,$then 

  $$\int_C \mathbf{f}\cdot \,d\mathbf{r} =\int_C P(x,y,z) \, dx +Q(x,y,z) \, dy +R(x,y,z) \, dz$$

* Work and Circulation
  $$W =\int_C \mathbf{f} \cdot d\mathbf{r} = \int_C \mathbf{f}\cdot \mathbf{T} \,ds $$
  $$\mathrm{Circulation}= \oint_C \mathbf{f} \cdot d\mathbf{r} = \oint_C \mathbf{f}\cdot \mathbf{T} \,ds $$

![](figures/ch09_figure21.svg "circulation"){width="45%" fig-align="center"}

